  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protection of Women&apos;s Rights in Afghanistan and Delivery of Essential Services to Address Gender-Based Violence (GBV)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Afghanistan is one of the most challenging environments for humanitarian action. Multiple interlocking shocks, including rapid economic collapse and sustained international isolation, have led to an increase in the needs of the population, currently estimated at over 44.5 million, including 6.3 million internally displaced persons (IDPs). 
 
This project aims to create a safe and supportive environment to protect the rights and dignity of women and girls, including IDPs, returnees and host community members. This is crucial to enable their access to safety and empowerment, including livelihood opportunities and the ability to build a supportive network across the identified locations. This is being achieved through the construction and/or refurbishment of women-friendly spaces in two locations, serving returnees at the border points and their areas of returns. The provision and maintenance of women and girl friendly spaces (WGFS) is a GBV minimum standard in emergencies. Given the sensitivities around terminology related to GBV response within the context, in external coordination and implementation, the WGFS will be referred to as rest areas for women and girls to limit any associated risks.</narrative>
  </description>
